WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.880
[Wilkes] Welcome to the MacMillan Report

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:25.269
I'm Marilyn Wilkes, your host, and today our guest is 
is Professor Alex Stone Sweet

00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:29.439
The Leitner Professor of Law, Politics and
International Studies,

00:00:29.439 --> 00:00:32.029
Professor Stone Sweet's interests are comparative

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:34.300
and international politics and law

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.310
and European integration.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:39.490
His research focuses on how rule systems emerge

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.019
and evolve over time

00:00:41.019 --> 00:00:43.860
and with what consequences for society.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:49.570
Most of his published work approaches this
question by looking at how new legal systems develop.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:53.809
Today, we'll be talking about his newest book
called "A Europe of Rights:

00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:56.319
The Impact of the European Convention

00:00:56.319 --> 00:01:02.140
on Human Rights on National Legal Systems".
Welcome, Professor Stone Sweet. 
[Stone Sweet] Thank you.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.490
[Wilkes] Most of your research focuses on new legal
systems and how they evolve.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:08.510
Why are you interested in that?

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:13.230
[Stone Sweet] Well, my work is little bit peculiar in
that most legal scholars and most political

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:14.450
scientists study

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.810
full-blown legal systems or legal systems that have already

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:22.860
matured and are already functioning, usually
in a successful way, and I'm interested in

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.490
brand new legal systems, because we don't know
if they're going to be

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:27.139
successes or failures

00:01:27.139 --> 00:01:28.720
and because when

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.590
people build new legal systems they're

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:35.270
constituting new political communities 
or new systems of governance

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:38.750
and when older, pre-existing political communities

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:42.670
redesign their legal systems, get new constitutions,
or set up new court systems,

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:44.690
they are redefining who they are,

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.650
politically and as a community. 
So I'm interested in

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:52.880
what are the

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.700
bases for when legal systems will be
successful

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.399
and when they'll fail, 
and what their functions are,

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:03.640
and it turns out that
legal systems,

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:06.500
and judges and courts in particular,
are critical to how

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.680
human communities are able to reproduce
themselves over time,

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:13.969
how societies are able to integrate
over time as circumstances change,

00:02:13.969 --> 00:02:16.630
how economies are able to grow and integrate.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:19.060
So for the last ten years or twelve years now

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.469
I've been working a lot on European immigration
and showing

00:02:22.469 --> 00:02:27.549
how important is has been
for integration

00:02:27.549 --> 00:02:29.929
particularly social and economic integration,

00:02:29.929 --> 00:02:32.939
how people interact across borders in Europe,

00:02:32.939 --> 00:02:35.280
how important the legal system has been

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.129
for the success of that project.

00:02:37.129 --> 00:02:41.159
On the contrary, in places like Africa
and in Latin America,

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:42.520
integration projects haven't worked,

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.150
and that's partly because

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.689
the legal systems have been so ineffective.

00:02:46.689 --> 00:02:50.269
[Wilkes] So what legal systems are you most interested
in today?

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:56.280
[Stone Sweet] Right now, I'm developing a project on 
private transnational governance

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:00.369
and I'm interested in how multinational corporations
and their lawyers

00:03:00.369 --> 00:03:05.219
are building a new legal system, but
a private legal system that

00:03:05.219 --> 00:03:09.319
doesn't rely on the state, to manage their
commercial relationships.

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:12.529
So almost all contracts now--

00:03:12.529 --> 00:03:13.620
Well, when Nike

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.830
has to make tennis shoes in Malaysia or
Vietnam, they have to have a contract with somebody

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:22.339
that is going to make those
shoes in those countries,

00:03:22.339 --> 00:03:27.549
and most of these kinds of contracts now specify

00:03:27.549 --> 00:03:32.159
arbitration to deal with contractual disputes
rather than court systems,

00:03:32.159 --> 00:03:35.119
and so through these arbitration clauses in contracts

00:03:35.119 --> 00:03:39.579
private companies are building a kind of
private court system for transnational commerce,

00:03:39.579 --> 00:03:44.950
and I find that fascinating because 
once again it allows us to look at the dynamics

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:47.399
of how new legal systems or new court systems evolve.

00:03:47.399 --> 00:03:49.539
Furthermore, in contracting,

00:03:49.539 --> 00:03:55.230
when multinational corporations
and their lawyers build new contracts,

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:59.010
often today, because they're put under pressure

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:01.779
from NGOs and interest groups that are interested in

00:04:01.779 --> 00:04:03.639
environmental protection or

00:04:03.639 --> 00:04:06.249
human rights records,

00:04:06.249 --> 00:04:10.489
they want these corporations to put directly
into the contracts human rights standards,

00:04:10.489 --> 00:04:13.719
workplace conditions, environmental protection
standards, and so on

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:16.410
and we find that corporations are doing
this more and more

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:19.949
which then raises the question of how these
standards, how these codes of conduct

00:04:19.949 --> 00:04:22.759
which again are purely private, 
they're not obligatory,

00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:26.750
are nonetheless being monitored 
and enforced. And then companies have

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:30.050
multiple ways of monitoring and enforcing it.
They can do it themselves,

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.219
they can hire an accounting firm

00:04:32.219 --> 00:04:37.889
like Coopers &amp; Lybrand or Andersen,
these huge accounting firms.

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:43.680
So you used to just audit the contract 
from its financial aspects

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.910
and nowadays, they do what's called social accounting

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.259
or environmental accounting: they look to see

00:04:48.259 --> 00:04:50.419
how the contractual relationship is doing,

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:54.409
is faring, on the human rights side or 
on the environmental protection side.

00:04:54.409 --> 00:04:57.290
Or a third way that they can do it, which is my
preferred way,

00:04:57.290 --> 00:05:01.320
is that they can ask a third party, a neutral
third party, to

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.460
go directly to, say, Vietnam or Malaysia

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:09.710
and look and see how the workplace is actually
being managed and then certify that workplace,

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:14.750
and the most important
or one of the most interesting

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.849
organizations that does this is called 
Social Accounting International

00:05:17.849 --> 00:05:19.129
and it's funded by

00:05:19.129 --> 00:05:20.490
labor and by

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:21.590
corporations themselves

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:25.520
and by NGOs and interest groups 
who are interested in human rights

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:26.509
and environmental protection.

00:05:26.509 --> 00:05:31.309
So I find this fascinating because
it's a kind of transnational

00:05:31.309 --> 00:05:32.400
legal system

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.370
that's purely private; it doesn't rely on the state

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:37.469
but it is increasingly important for managing
global

00:05:37.469 --> 00:05:39.329
economic activity.

00:05:39.329 --> 00:05:43.240
[Wilkes] So tell us about your new book, and what
is the European convention?

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:46.639
Well the European Convention on Human Rights was
signed by ten states

00:05:46.639 --> 00:05:49.310
in 1953 and it entered into force

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:51.270
in 1953.

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:55.499
Today, it covers 47 countries in Europe

00:05:55.499 --> 00:05:58.610
and 850 million people.

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:04.460
So it's also the most active and most effective international
human rights regime in the world.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.480
Last year it received 50,000

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.229
--more than 50,000 petitions--from individuals

00:06:09.229 --> 00:06:10.639
directly to it, and

00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:15.669
it issued more than 1,500 rulings.

00:06:15.669 --> 00:06:17.449
It's the single most active court in the world.

00:06:17.449 --> 00:06:20.130
To give you a comparison,

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:22.209
the Supreme Court of the United States

00:06:22.209 --> 00:06:26.989
issues fewer than 100 rulings per year.
This court, this transnational court,

00:06:26.989 --> 00:06:30.340
made up of 47 judges from 47 different countries

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:35.199
issues 1,500+ decisions a year on the merits.

00:06:35.199 --> 00:06:38.449
[Wilkes] Wow, that's fascinating.

00:06:38.449 --> 00:06:41.629
That's quite a bit of research to follow, I would imagine.

00:06:41.629 --> 00:06:46.909
Yes, the new book traces,
or attempts to trace, the impact

00:06:46.909 --> 00:06:50.509
of the European Convention on Human Rights
and how it's interpreted

00:06:50.509 --> 00:06:52.560
by the European Court of Human Rights

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.560
on the member states themselves, on the nation
states themselves, the contracting states.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:00.020
So instead of a focus where we look just at
the European Court of Human Rights

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:01.870
and what it does and how it interprets rights,

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:03.279
we care about how

00:07:03.279 --> 00:07:07.340
legislators, and governments,

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:07.699
administrators,

00:07:07.699 --> 00:07:09.029
and judges

00:07:09.029 --> 00:07:10.849
receive or

00:07:10.849 --> 00:07:12.300
adapt to

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.939
or coordinate their activities, their 
decision-making

00:07:15.939 --> 00:07:18.960
to the rights that are in the convention

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:20.779
as they're interpreted over time

00:07:20.779 --> 00:07:23.819
by the European Convention on Human Rights. 
Now the convention

00:07:23.819 --> 00:07:26.020
completely changed in 1998

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:28.229
when the states decided

00:07:28.229 --> 00:07:31.979
to make the jurisdiction of the

00:07:31.979 --> 00:07:33.739
European court compulsory.

00:07:33.739 --> 00:07:34.900
By that, it means that

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:37.789
states can't block

00:07:37.789 --> 00:07:40.970
applications that come from their own citizens
to the court against them

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:42.159
from going to court,

00:07:42.159 --> 00:07:44.090
and they also made a

00:07:45.629 --> 00:07:48.689
right of all citizens, or for that matter

00:07:48.689 --> 00:07:50.689
all inhabitants of

00:07:50.689 --> 00:07:54.729
the convention territory, that is all forty
seven countries,

00:07:54.729 --> 00:07:58.530
they gave an absolute right to individuals
to petition directly the court

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:01.539
when they believe that their rights had
been violated

00:08:01.539 --> 00:08:04.019
after remedies have been exhausted in those
countries.

00:08:04.019 --> 00:08:08.919
So in 1998 then, the court really became 
a kind of transnational constitutional

00:08:08.919 --> 00:08:11.209
court for human rights

00:08:11.209 --> 00:08:12.659
in the convention.

00:08:12.659 --> 00:08:16.780
[Wilkes] Okay, so how much impact has the European Convention
on Human Rights

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:21.880
actually had on national political and legal
systems? 
[Stone Sweet] Well in the last twenty years, and particularly

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:27.729
(and this has accelerated in the last decade,
especially since the changes in 1998 that I just mentioned)

00:08:27.729 --> 00:08:31.199
the court's impact has become really profound

00:08:31.199 --> 00:08:33.310
across all the contracting states.

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:37.060
Of course, some states have been influenced
more than others

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:41.430
Those states that have very, very, very good
human rights records

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:43.650
with good systems of constitutional justice

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:45.650
with constitutional courts that protect rights

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:51.240
fairly comprehensively in the national legal
order, such as Germany, and Ireland, and Spain,

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:53.530
they haven't been impacted as much.

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:55.700
The convention doesn't fill those gaps.

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:57.340
But in other systems

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:00.680
where they don't have a bill of rights and 
a constitution
0:09:00.6800:09:02.329
and most countries, actually

00:09:02.329 --> 00:09:05.619
in the old Western Europe, didn't have a bill
of rights in their countries

00:09:05.619 --> 00:09:07.610
and they didn't have judicial review.

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:08.609
That is, they didn't have,

00:09:08.609 --> 00:09:10.220
judges didn't have the power

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:13.890
of striking down or annulling as unconstitutional

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:20.560
statutes that emanated out of the parliament. Parliaments
were sovereign, parliaments were supreme

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:23.540
and the acts that they produced, legislation,
statutes,

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:25.490
were supreme in the legal order.

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:29.120
In those systems, there's been profound change
because the convention

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:34.700
now functions as a kind of surrogate
bill of rights, it becomes the bill of rights

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:37.410
and to the extent to which judges can directly
enforce them

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:40.490
is the extent to which the convention starts
to have direct effect

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:41.640
in the legal system.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:44.440
In most systems now since 1998,

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:47.170
in most systems, there's only a couple of exceptions,

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:49.839
the convention has been incorporated

00:09:49.839 --> 00:09:51.410
into the national legal system

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:53.440
at a level that's above statutes

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:54.470
above legislation

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:56.650
the below that of the constitution.

00:09:56.650 --> 00:09:58.229
What that means is that

00:09:58.229 --> 00:09:59.410
individuals can plead

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:04.610
rights from the convention directly
in national courts

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:05.040
against their own national government

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:08.490
and judges are expected to enforce them, even
again statutes.

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:12.190
So again, what this means is that the
way the convention has developed

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:13.620
and the way that

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:21.450
the convention has been incorporated into national
law--that is, with that super-legislative status--means that

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:26.519
the convention

00:10:26.519 --> 00:10:30.500
functions as a kind of constitutional
bill of rights for all those states

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:31.950
that didn't have it before.

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:37.000
[Wilkes] So your book shows that the convention system
is presently at a critical juncture.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:41.410
What are the major challenges facing the European
Court on Human Rights today?

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:42.870
Well, in 1990

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:45.100
the convention system

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:46.769
added 24 new states

00:10:46.769 --> 00:10:48.760
with 450 million people.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:51.690
This is called "eastern enlargement". This happened
after

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:55.990
the fall of the Berlin Wall and the end, 
the disintegration of the Soviet bloc.

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:59.920
All of those countries east of Western Europe,
east of the European community

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:01.090
and European Union,

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:05.790
drafted new constitutions, 
were engaged in

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:06.719
democratization efforts,

00:11:06.719 --> 00:11:08.110
and the Council of Europe,

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:12.309
of which the convention is a part,

00:11:12.309 --> 00:11:16.459
decided--the political body, the states in the Council of Europe,
the Western European states--decided that it

00:11:16.459 --> 00:11:17.420
was important

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:20.980
to bring Eastern Europe into the Western
European

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:22.490
into the convention system

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:24.690
in order to 

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:27.290
bolster democratization efforts, in order to
give them

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:34.920
to provide a bulwark for these
new systems so that they wouldn't slide back into

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:37.150
authoritarianism and so on,

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:40.100
and one of the problems has been that some
of these states

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.040
were not really ready

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:43.539
to be brought into

00:11:43.539 --> 00:11:44.920
this new system,

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:46.990
and this is certainly this case of

00:11:46.990 --> 00:11:48.249
the Ukraine

00:11:48.249 --> 00:11:50.500
Georgia, Russia, Turkey,

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:53.070
Romania, Bulgaria.

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:57.659
Today, more than half

00:11:57.659 --> 00:12:02.659
of all the 50,000 petitions that come
in each year come from only four states:

00:12:02.659 --> 00:12:04.220
Romania, Ukraine,

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:05.380
Russia, and Turkey.

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:09.950
In these places, you have massive human rights
failures of a systemic kind. You have judges

00:12:09.950 --> 00:12:11.770
who are underpaid or corrupt.

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:13.420
You have judges who are afraid

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:16.750
of political reprisals if they do their
job correctly.

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:18.940
You have governments who actually

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:19.910
censor the mail

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:21.220
and if they see mail--

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:23.500
Uh, I should back up.

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:26.830
Individuals can apply directly to
the conventions through a letter, basically.

00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:28.970
It's incredibly simple and costless

00:12:28.970 --> 00:12:30.930
to register one of these complaints,

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:33.450
and some governments, including Russia, have
actually

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:35.569
stopped mail going

00:12:35.569 --> 00:12:37.139
out of Russia to

00:12:37.139 --> 00:12:40.379
the Strasbourg Court, to the European Court of
Human Rights, because they don't want

00:12:40.379 --> 00:12:42.650
the European Court to handle some of these kinds of complaints.

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:46.700
And a further problem is, as you know
if you've been watching the news,

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:51.430
Russia, for instance, is engaged in

00:12:51.430 --> 00:12:53.780
in virtual civil war in Chechnya,

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:55.670
and it's been engaged in

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:58.290
some military action in Georgia,

00:12:58.290 --> 00:13:02.730
all of those actions will generate

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:05.240
many complaints and petitions that will all come
to the court

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:07.780
and unlike the American Supreme Court which
has a

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:09.789
political questions doctrine and has

00:13:09.789 --> 00:13:14.499
a set of other doctrines that says that
when we're dealing with foreign policy and war issues,

00:13:14.499 --> 00:13:17.329
think of Guantanamo, we defer to our President.

00:13:17.329 --> 00:13:20.190
We don't really want to apply the same standards

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:22.330
that we might apply to government action

00:13:22.330 --> 00:13:25.449
within America to what the government does
outside of America.

00:13:25.449 --> 00:13:28.920
Well the European Court of Human Rights never does that. There
is no political question.

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:30.060
Every question,

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:34.760
according to the court, every
question needs to be

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:38.950
examined and dealt with on its merits,
according to the law and according

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:40.619
to the convention, and that means that

00:13:40.619 --> 00:13:43.830
no matter how political or how controversial a
question is

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:45.850
that comes up in any of these 47
states,

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:48.470
the court is almost certain to get it.

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:50.580
At the same time, the court knows

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:52.150
that when it's dealing with some of these

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:54.149
cases, some of these

00:13:54.149 --> 00:13:55.210
difficult cases

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:57.130
of countries that

00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:58.220
are unable

00:13:58.220 --> 00:13:59.960
to protect human rights

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:00.909
on their own

00:14:00.909 --> 00:14:02.300
through their own domestic

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:05.149
processes, through their own domestic institutions,

00:14:05.149 --> 00:14:06.280
they know that

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:11.460
that their capacity to actually impact
on them is quite low.

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:12.900
So, the court can

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:14.300
continually handle

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:17.320
hundreds and hundreds, sometimes thousands of the same
kinds of complaints

00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:22.189
and give direction to the Russian authorities or to the
Ukrainian authorities on how the need to change their

00:14:22.189 --> 00:14:25.019
system in order to stop generating these kinds
of complaints,

00:14:25.019 --> 00:14:27.159
but they also can know that

00:14:27.159 --> 00:14:29.220
the Russians and the Ukrainians are unlikely

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:30.660
to succeed at

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:32.650
reforming their own institutions.

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:35.740
[Wilkes] Thank you for sharing some of your work with
us today.

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:39.199
For more information on Professor Stone Sweet,

00:14:39.199 --> 00:14:41.700
his research, and of course, his newest book,

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:47.050
please visit our web site at www.yale.edu/macmillanreport

00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:52.170
Be sure to join us again for another episode
of the MacMillan Report, made possible

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:55.769
through finding from the 
Whitney and Betty MacMillan Center

00:14:55.769 --> 00:14:59.300
for International and Area Studies at Yale.

00:14:59.300 --> 00:14:59.550
Thank you very much.

